{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "003D", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{A}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{B}$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0004": "$$\n\\circ : \\Ob(\\text{Fun}(\\mathcal{B}, \\mathcal{C}))\n\\times\n\\Ob(\\text{Fun}(\\mathcal{A}, \\mathcal{B}))\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Ob(\\text{Fun}(\\mathcal{A}, \\mathcal{C}))\n$$", "MATH_0005": "$\\textit{Cat}$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\n\\medskip\\noindent\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:354b6b5a8d9b3c235e7b373024b18e12ad9351e087e493d99d0d6e4bc7a41e85", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:7260784b88270e773412b075fbc5b898009a803b1a140d41c62d4e5a1ae1d649", "source_text": "Namely for every triple of categories <MATH_0001>, <MATH_0002>, and <MATH_0003> there is a composition law <MATH_0004> coming from composition of functors. This composition law is associative, and identity functors act as units. In other words -- forgetting about transformations of functors -- we see that <MATH_0005> forms a category. How does this structure interact with the morphisms between functors?", "source_text_hash": "sha256:97a4f3a1910d2c186469635353ce0da657c96018aad7122dcf083f6f265b9ff1", "unit_id": "tag:003D:p003"}
